WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.040 --> 00:00:08.392
- Hello, I'm Alice Kaplan, director of

00:00:08.392 --> 00:00:10.910
the Whitney Humanities Center.

00:00:10.910 --> 00:00:15.870
Welcome to the spring 2021
Finzi-Contini Lecture.

00:00:15.870 --> 00:00:19.610
Before Meghan O'Rourke introduces
our distinguished speaker,

00:00:19.610 --> 00:00:23.690
Hernan Diaz, I'd like to say
a few words about the history

00:00:23.690 --> 00:00:27.140
and the tradition behind today's event.

00:00:27.140 --> 00:00:31.530
The Finzi-Contini Lectureship
was endowed in 1990 by

00:00:31.530 --> 00:00:34.700
the Honorable Guido Calabresi, judge of

00:00:34.700 --> 00:00:37.670
the US Court of Appeals
for the Second Circuit

00:00:37.670 --> 00:00:40.173
and former Dean of the Yale Law School.

00:00:42.060 --> 00:00:43.980
It was also endowed by his brother

00:00:43.980 --> 00:00:46.870
the late Dr. Paul Calabresi.

00:00:46.870 --> 00:00:49.230
This lecture honors their mother,

00:00:49.230 --> 00:00:52.990
Bianca Maria Finzi-Contini Calabresi.

00:00:52.990 --> 00:00:56.230
She and her husband
Massimo, early activists in

00:00:56.230 --> 00:01:00.977
the resistance to Italian
fascism fled Italy in 1939 after

00:01:01.990 --> 00:01:05.560
the passage of the first
infamous racial laws.

00:01:05.560 --> 00:01:09.660
They settled in New Haven to
start a new he as a physician,

00:01:09.660 --> 00:01:12.220
she as a literary scholar.

00:01:12.220 --> 00:01:16.190
Bianca Maria Finzi-Contini
earned her PhD in French

00:01:16.190 --> 00:01:20.300
at Yale in 1949 with a dissertation on

00:01:20.300 --> 00:01:25.300
the scholarly influences an
Ernest Renan secular upbringing.

00:01:25.430 --> 00:01:29.440
Her subject Renan is one of
the most fascinating writers

00:01:29.440 --> 00:01:31.230
of the 19th Century.

00:01:31.230 --> 00:01:34.970
A free thinker and brilliant
analyst of nationalism

00:01:34.970 --> 00:01:38.870
and of Christianity who
believed in racial hierarchies

00:01:38.870 --> 00:01:41.660
but nonetheless counted among his enemies

00:01:41.660 --> 00:01:45.290
the most vicious antisemites in France.

00:01:45.290 --> 00:01:49.060
Lore has it that Professor
Ali Pyle, the director

00:01:49.060 --> 00:01:52.070
of Bianca Finzi-Contini's thesis wanted

00:01:52.070 --> 00:01:54.890
to appoint her to the faculty at Yale but

00:01:54.890 --> 00:01:59.140
the 1949 Yale Corporation refused.

00:01:59.140 --> 00:02:02.520
There would be no tenured
woman on the faculty at Yale

00:02:02.520 --> 00:02:04.433
for another full decade.

00:02:05.430 --> 00:02:08.880
Bianca Maria Finzi-Contini
went on to become professor

00:02:08.880 --> 00:02:11.850
of French and Italian
at Connecticut College,

00:02:11.850 --> 00:02:14.280
then chair of the department of Italian

00:02:14.280 --> 00:02:17.140
at Albertus Magnus College.

00:02:17.140 --> 00:02:20.500
Her last published volume was
also written in French like

00:02:20.500 --> 00:02:24.470
her dissertation and it was
on Renan's relations with one

00:02:24.470 --> 00:02:27.600
of the founders of classic philology.

00:02:27.600 --> 00:02:31.363
She died in 1982 at the age of 80.

00:02:32.490 --> 00:02:36.620
I'd like to take this opportunity
to thank Judge Calabresi

00:02:36.620 --> 00:02:40.300
and his family for their
generosity and for giving us

00:02:40.300 --> 00:02:44.310
the privilege of remembering
a distinguished alumni,

00:02:44.310 --> 00:02:46.663
Bianca Maria Finzi-Contini.

00:02:47.730 --> 00:02:51.580
The Finzi-Contini Lectures
sponsors a distinguished speaker

00:02:51.580 --> 00:02:56.140
in the field of comparative
literature broadly defined.

00:02:56.140 --> 00:02:59.710
Over the years the Whitney
has welcomed translators,

00:02:59.710 --> 00:03:03.940
editors, theorists and
practitioners of literature.

00:03:03.940 --> 00:03:08.550
The inaugural lecture in the
series was delivered in 1991 by

00:03:08.550 --> 00:03:12.463
the great Italian semiotician Umberto Eco.

00:03:13.610 --> 00:03:16.790
This year for the first time
the Whitney Humanities Center

00:03:16.790 --> 00:03:21.310
is joining with The Yale
Review as co-sponsors of

00:03:21.310 --> 00:03:23.420
the Finzi-Contini lecture.

00:03:23.420 --> 00:03:27.140
It's my great pleasure now
to introduce the editor

00:03:27.140 --> 00:03:31.380
of The Yale Review, Meghan
O'Rourke, who took over

00:03:31.380 --> 00:03:35.020
the editorship of The
Yale Review in early 2020

00:03:35.020 --> 00:03:39.273
as the magazine was celebrating
its 200th anniversary.

00:03:40.220 --> 00:03:43.850
A graduate of Yale, Meghan
is an accomplished poet

00:03:43.850 --> 00:03:47.190
and essayist, a former
editor at The New Yorker,

00:03:47.190 --> 00:03:49.490
the Paris Review and Slate.

00:03:49.490 --> 00:03:51.667
She's the author of
the bestselling memoir,

00:03:51.667 --> 00:03:55.510
"The Long Goodbye" and
three poetry collections.

00:03:55.510 --> 00:03:59.550
She's published essays on the
love poems of Thomas Hardy,

00:03:59.550 --> 00:04:04.410
on Guantanamo, on John Ashbery
and on the early history

00:04:04.410 --> 00:04:07.320
of young adult detective fiction.

00:04:07.320 --> 00:04:10.890
She has spent this pandemic
year finishing her next book

00:04:10.890 --> 00:04:15.250
of nonfiction on
appropriately enough illness.

00:04:15.250 --> 00:04:18.650
She's the recipient of
many literary awards

00:04:18.650 --> 00:04:21.850
including a Guggenheim,
a Lannan Fellowship

00:04:21.850 --> 00:04:24.350
and two Pushcart Prizes.

00:04:24.350 --> 00:04:28.920
So Meghan, I'm passing the
floor or the square to you

00:04:28.920 --> 00:04:31.840
to introduce Hernan Diaz.

00:04:31.840 --> 00:04:34.530
- Thank you, and sorry,
I clicked on too early

00:04:34.530 --> 00:04:38.980
but all of nicer to share
the screen with you Alice.

00:04:38.980 --> 00:04:41.000
Good afternoon everyone.

00:04:41.000 --> 00:04:44.060
I am extremely pleased
to welcome Hernan Diaz

00:04:44.060 --> 00:04:46.910
to give the Finzi-Contini lecture today at

00:04:46.910 --> 00:04:48.020
the Whitney Humanities Center.

00:04:48.020 --> 00:04:50.180
And I want to thank Alice
Kaplan and the center

00:04:50.180 --> 00:04:53.340
for inviting The Yale
Review to be a co-sponsor

00:04:53.340 --> 00:04:54.530
of this event.

00:04:54.530 --> 00:04:57.150
We're very excited to say
that we'll be publishing

00:04:58.501 --> 00:05:01.400
Renan's lecture in print
in our summer issue

00:05:01.400 --> 00:05:03.810
which I'll remind you
of at the end so we hope

00:05:03.810 --> 00:05:05.943
you'll subscribe or get a copy.

00:05:07.341 --> 00:05:10.010
Okay, Hernan Diaz is the author of a study

00:05:10.010 --> 00:05:11.660
of Jorge Luis Borges.

00:05:11.660 --> 00:05:14.027
The Argentine essayist and poet titled,

00:05:14.027 --> 00:05:17.390
"Borges, between History
and Eternity" as well

00:05:17.390 --> 00:05:19.390
as the brilliant novel "In The Distance".

00:05:19.390 --> 00:05:22.170
A finalist for the 2018 Pulitzer Prize and

00:05:22.170 --> 00:05:26.980
the PEN/Faulkner Award among
many other citations that year.

00:05:26.980 --> 00:05:29.970
In this novel, Diaz
turns the conventions of

00:05:29.970 --> 00:05:33.910
the American Western on their
head, subverting expectations

00:05:33.910 --> 00:05:35.640
and writing a work of fiction that in

00:05:35.640 --> 00:05:39.660
the end becomes a profound
meditation on storytelling

00:05:39.660 --> 00:05:43.170
on both the intimate and national scale.

00:05:43.170 --> 00:05:45.990
Well, westerns are typically
set after the civil war,

00:05:45.990 --> 00:05:47.740
this one is set before it.

00:05:47.740 --> 00:05:50.950
And its hero is a recent
immigrant from Sweden,

00:05:50.950 --> 00:05:54.720
Hakan Soderstrom, who doesn't
speak English from much

00:05:54.720 --> 00:05:58.410
of the novel as he journeys
across America in search

00:05:58.410 --> 00:06:00.380
of his lost older brother.

00:06:00.380 --> 00:06:03.560
A journey that takes an
eastward toward New York city

00:06:03.560 --> 00:06:06.510
rather than westward away from it.

00:06:06.510 --> 00:06:09.240
In an interview, Diaz
referred to his novel

00:06:09.240 --> 00:06:12.570
as a "Western Without Cowboys".

00:06:12.570 --> 00:06:15.260
And noted that, "I
thought it was important

00:06:15.260 --> 00:06:18.580
to quite literally
alienate the genre in order

00:06:18.580 --> 00:06:21.790
to question the ossified
political assumptions

00:06:21.790 --> 00:06:23.047
at its core."

00:06:24.410 --> 00:06:27.650
The subverted western frame
in the distance asks us

00:06:27.650 --> 00:06:31.670
to think about what and who
has been counted as American

00:06:31.670 --> 00:06:34.850
in a history that has
whitewashed its own violence

00:06:34.850 --> 00:06:38.920
and holds a productive
tension for the reader ideas

00:06:38.920 --> 00:06:41.570
of foreignness and ideas of connection

00:06:41.570 --> 00:06:45.280
as when Hakan, adrift
in a new world searching

00:06:45.280 --> 00:06:48.360
for connection meets a scientist
who teaches him English

00:06:48.360 --> 00:06:51.040
and introduces him to
the notion that the bond

00:06:51.040 --> 00:06:54.900
between all living things
is the "true religion."

00:06:54.900 --> 00:06:58.280
Most of all though, this becomes
a discerning astute frame

00:06:59.660 --> 00:07:02.240
for thinking about why
we tell stories at all

00:07:02.240 --> 00:07:04.390
and how they comfort us.

00:07:04.390 --> 00:07:07.310
Over the course of the novel
Diaz reflects on this question

00:07:07.310 --> 00:07:10.687
very profoundly, very
movingly, very voluminously.

00:07:12.420 --> 00:07:16.100
And at one point we meet
Hakan reflecting on the fact

00:07:16.100 --> 00:07:18.270
that his brother whom he's
missing that his story

00:07:18.270 --> 00:07:20.760
is "endow the world with meaning" and

00:07:20.760 --> 00:07:23.320
that through these
stories life was contained

00:07:23.320 --> 00:07:27.023
becoming something that could
be examined and understood.

00:07:27.910 --> 00:07:31.710
In today's talk, Diaz examines
the art of fiction in order

00:07:31.710 --> 00:07:34.590
to help us better understand
how it offers us truths

00:07:34.590 --> 00:07:37.930
we cannot find elsewhere
and how reading rather

00:07:37.930 --> 00:07:41.580
than research might best help
the writer of fiction achieve

00:07:41.580 --> 00:07:44.703
the goals of building a
persuasive new reality.

00:07:46.420 --> 00:07:49.040
Diaz serves as an associate director of

00:07:49.040 --> 00:07:52.230
the Hispanic Institute
at Columbia University.

00:07:52.230 --> 00:07:54.630
Born in Argentina, he
was raised in Sweden.

00:07:54.630 --> 00:07:58.040
Studied in London and New
York and now lives in Brooklyn

00:07:58.040 --> 00:07:59.920
with his wife and daughter.

00:07:59.920 --> 00:08:03.130
I very much recommend that
you read his novel it's one

00:08:03.130 --> 00:08:05.313
of my favorite books of the last years.

00:08:06.170 --> 00:08:08.750
Tonight he's going to
speak to us about fiction

00:08:09.640 --> 00:08:12.610
for about 40 minutes and then
we'll take questions from

00:08:12.610 --> 00:08:14.970
the audience and from me for about 10.

00:08:14.970 --> 00:08:17.410
To ask a question and
I'll remind you of this at

00:08:17.410 --> 00:08:20.870
the end we ask that you
please use the Q and A feature

00:08:20.870 --> 00:08:22.543
at the bottom of the screen.

00:08:23.460 --> 00:08:26.440
In the meantime please join me
in giving a very warm welcome

00:08:26.440 --> 00:08:27.750
to Hernan Diaz.

00:08:27.750 --> 00:08:28.903
Thank you so much.

00:08:30.860 --> 00:08:32.470
- Hi Meghan.

00:08:32.470 --> 00:08:37.470
Thank you for your extremely
generous introduction.

00:08:38.080 --> 00:08:42.080
Thank you also Alice Kaplan, Diane Brown,

00:08:42.080 --> 00:08:44.710
the Whitney Humanities Center and everyone

00:08:44.710 --> 00:08:47.650
at The Yale Review for
this wonderful invitation.

00:08:47.650 --> 00:08:52.650
And thank you all for being
here in one way or another.

00:08:53.390 --> 00:08:57.170
It's an overwhelming
honor to be delivering

00:08:57.170 --> 00:09:01.740
the Finzi-Contini lecture
and I'm truly humbling to see

00:09:01.740 --> 00:09:05.863
so many of my heroes in the archives.

00:09:07.220 --> 00:09:09.717
Alice just mentioned Umberto
Eco but Tzvetan Todorov,

00:09:09.717 --> 00:09:14.717
Charles Rosen, W.S. Merwin are in there

00:09:14.970 --> 00:09:19.463
and it's really an immense honor.

00:09:20.910 --> 00:09:23.430
I would also like to thank
my friends Paul Stacey

00:09:23.430 --> 00:09:26.370
and Eunice Rodriguez Ferguson
for the many conversations

00:09:26.370 --> 00:09:30.340
I had with them as I wrote this text.

00:09:30.340 --> 00:09:34.750
And I should also say that
Meghan's invitation arrived

00:09:34.750 --> 00:09:38.350
as I was starting to work
with my editor on a novel

00:09:38.350 --> 00:09:39.683
I have just finished.

00:09:41.210 --> 00:09:46.210
This new book is very much
about the notion of fiction.

00:09:46.260 --> 00:09:47.890
What does fiction mean?

00:09:47.890 --> 00:09:52.010
How do stories shape our
perception of reality?

00:09:52.010 --> 00:09:55.993
How can fiction alter and
even constitute reality?

00:09:56.910 --> 00:10:00.370
I thought it would be productive
to present these questions

00:10:00.370 --> 00:10:05.370
in an essay stick form after
having written the novel.

00:10:05.800 --> 00:10:09.680
The essay has nothing to
do with the novel plot wise

00:10:09.680 --> 00:10:13.190
of course because as you'll
hear in a moment this

00:10:13.190 --> 00:10:15.640
is a concern I've had for many years now.

00:10:15.640 --> 00:10:19.850
What does fiction say about the world and

00:10:19.850 --> 00:10:22.630
the many ways in which we experience it

00:10:22.630 --> 00:10:27.323
and moreover how does
fiction fit in the world?

00:10:28.650 --> 00:10:32.120
So this talk will be as
Meghan said 40 minutes

00:10:32.120 --> 00:10:35.803
and it's divided into five sections.

00:10:36.690 --> 00:10:41.690
The title is of course a loving
pun on Henry James's essay

00:10:41.967 --> 00:10:46.943
"The Art of Fiction" which
I'll reference in a moment.

00:10:49.740 --> 00:10:51.163
The art of fiction.

00:10:52.550 --> 00:10:57.550
Fiction writers are a famously
neurotic bunch haunted

00:10:57.660 --> 00:10:58.843
by self doubt.

00:10:59.710 --> 00:11:03.500
From time to time, this
unease spreads and swells

00:11:03.500 --> 00:11:07.340
into a general state of
disquiet and even shock

00:11:07.340 --> 00:11:11.490
as a fiction itself suddenly
realize to its dismay

00:11:11.490 --> 00:11:13.203
that it's just fiction.

00:11:14.330 --> 00:11:17.240
Why dwell on made up stories?

00:11:17.240 --> 00:11:20.290
Why make them up in the first place?

00:11:20.290 --> 00:11:23.210
One response has often
been to present fiction

00:11:23.210 --> 00:11:26.400
as something useful, a
fabrication certainly

00:11:26.400 --> 00:11:29.820
but one redeemed by serving a purpose.

00:11:29.820 --> 00:11:33.880
Say religious instruction,
moral edification,

00:11:33.880 --> 00:11:37.333
political enlightenment
or even self discovery.

00:11:38.640 --> 00:11:43.420
The opposite response claiming
that stories serve no purpose

00:11:43.420 --> 00:11:46.600
at all is just as common.

00:11:46.600 --> 00:11:51.120
Fiction is an invention, yes,
but it's completely harmless,

00:11:51.120 --> 00:11:55.970
irrelevant play, a pastime
detached from real life not

00:11:55.970 --> 00:11:57.623
to be taken seriously.

00:11:59.610 --> 00:12:04.610
The latest symptom of fiction's
tendency towards self doubt

00:12:04.870 --> 00:12:09.690
is a more or less pervasive
referential fetish

00:12:09.690 --> 00:12:13.550
as if a strict adherence
to verifiable facts

00:12:13.550 --> 00:12:15.723
could dissipate this anxiety.

00:12:16.840 --> 00:12:19.610
I'd be enabled to say
when this first started

00:12:19.610 --> 00:12:22.830
but literature fixation
with accuracy seems

00:12:22.830 --> 00:12:25.193
to be a relatively new phenomenon.

00:12:26.150 --> 00:12:29.120
I wonder for instance when fiction started

00:12:29.120 --> 00:12:31.330
to be fact-checked.

00:12:31.330 --> 00:12:34.540
This impulse overlaps with the
thought that literature ought

00:12:34.540 --> 00:12:38.090
to be researched an eloquent example of

00:12:38.090 --> 00:12:40.380
the referential fetish.

00:12:40.380 --> 00:12:43.928
Yet the notion of
research implies arriving

00:12:43.928 --> 00:12:47.950
at verifiable results
through scientific protocols

00:12:47.950 --> 00:12:51.680
and methods that don't
translate all that well into

00:12:51.680 --> 00:12:53.183
the realm of fiction.

00:12:54.120 --> 00:12:56.980
There is actually a much
better word for this process

00:12:56.980 --> 00:12:59.203
and literature, reading.

00:13:00.680 --> 00:13:02.930
Differentiating between these two terms

00:13:02.930 --> 00:13:04.293
is not a mere quibble.

00:13:05.920 --> 00:13:10.070
The free associations,
the haphazard hermeneutics

00:13:10.070 --> 00:13:13.040
and perhaps above all
the emotional dimension

00:13:13.040 --> 00:13:17.940
of reading have nothing
to do with research.

00:13:17.940 --> 00:13:20.910
I would even claim that what
makes reading so compelling

00:13:20.910 --> 00:13:25.700
and productive is precisely
it's lack of method

00:13:25.700 --> 00:13:30.500
and yet the desire to get
it right which I think tends

00:13:30.500 --> 00:13:33.330
to go well beyond the reasonable desire

00:13:33.330 --> 00:13:36.490
of saving oneself from the
occasional embarrassment

00:13:36.490 --> 00:13:39.803
of getting it wrong often prevails.

00:13:40.730 --> 00:13:44.863
I see this happen to fellow
writers, I see it happen to me.

00:13:47.020 --> 00:13:50.800
Another manifestation of
the referential fetish

00:13:50.800 --> 00:13:54.740
is the increasingly strong
link between fiction

00:13:54.740 --> 00:13:57.900
and confession or witnessing.

00:13:57.900 --> 00:14:02.510
Consider autofiction and
other literatures of the self

00:14:02.510 --> 00:14:07.130
that have gained so much
prominence over the last few years.

00:14:07.130 --> 00:14:10.950
Here, that mysterious maxim
according to which one

00:14:10.950 --> 00:14:14.120
should write about what one knows is taken

00:14:14.120 --> 00:14:16.493
to solipsistic extremes.

00:14:17.780 --> 00:14:20.410
The testimonial nature of these books

00:14:20.410 --> 00:14:23.930
is not I believe unrelated
to the general sense

00:14:23.930 --> 00:14:27.510
of anxiety around the
place of truth and fiction

00:14:27.510 --> 00:14:30.120
and provides a hedge against the demands

00:14:30.120 --> 00:14:32.840
of accuracy and research.

00:14:32.840 --> 00:14:37.050
What is experienced, witnessed
or remembered need not

00:14:37.050 --> 00:14:40.030
be corroborated by other means.

00:14:40.030 --> 00:14:43.750
But differently, a perceived
lack of objectivity

00:14:43.750 --> 00:14:47.433
is offset by a surplus of subjectivity.

00:14:49.560 --> 00:14:54.380
The literature of the self
offers the promise of immediacy

00:14:54.380 --> 00:14:57.950
and presents the first
person where it surrogate

00:14:57.950 --> 00:15:00.800
as the guarantor of truth.

00:15:00.800 --> 00:15:04.653
I was there, I did it, I remember it.

00:15:05.960 --> 00:15:08.530
Eating a madeleine
however has never turned

00:15:08.530 --> 00:15:10.423
anyone into Proust.

00:15:11.690 --> 00:15:15.470
Even if there are many texts
in this autofictional vein

00:15:15.470 --> 00:15:19.190
that I admire, I must ultimately
side with Mary McCarthy

00:15:19.190 --> 00:15:22.830
who despite often smudging
the boundaries between fiction

00:15:22.830 --> 00:15:26.070
and autobiography in
her own work declared in

00:15:26.070 --> 00:15:28.730
the company she keeps with a brilliant pun

00:15:28.730 --> 00:15:33.100
that "the home address of the self is not

00:15:33.100 --> 00:15:34.857
to be found in the book".

00:15:36.260 --> 00:15:40.230
Yet it seems that autofiction's
promise of closeness

00:15:40.230 --> 00:15:44.770
and veracity has something to
do with the current prominence

00:15:44.770 --> 00:15:47.053
of these kinds of texts.

00:15:48.600 --> 00:15:52.970
The referential fetish then
seems to be an overcompensation

00:15:52.970 --> 00:15:57.173
for the uneasy relationship
between fiction and truth.

00:15:58.020 --> 00:16:01.200
This current obsession is not unrelated

00:16:01.200 --> 00:16:04.920
to the alarming turn a public
discourse started taking

00:16:04.920 --> 00:16:06.423
some four years ago.

00:16:07.290 --> 00:16:10.870
We all know what happened,
incontestable facts,

00:16:10.870 --> 00:16:13.450
scientific reports, election results

00:16:13.450 --> 00:16:17.290
and even daily events
verifiable through our senses

00:16:17.290 --> 00:16:19.793
were exiled from the realm of truth.

00:16:20.930 --> 00:16:25.060
Meanwhile, the most outlandish
narratives were presented

00:16:25.060 --> 00:16:27.453
as irrefutable reality.

00:16:28.730 --> 00:16:33.000
I don't intend to discuss
here the damage this has done

00:16:33.000 --> 00:16:37.510
to a number of institutions
and how it has eroded public

00:16:37.510 --> 00:16:39.480
trust in them.

00:16:39.480 --> 00:16:42.900
This has been the object of
abundant coverage elsewhere.

00:16:42.900 --> 00:16:47.823
My concern rather is what
it has done to fiction.

00:16:48.700 --> 00:16:52.610
Increasingly over the
past few years I've heard

00:16:52.610 --> 00:16:55.490
and read a growing
number of people say they

00:16:55.490 --> 00:16:58.730
have lost interest in fiction.

00:16:58.730 --> 00:17:02.180
Reality had become so
daunting and the proliferation

00:17:02.180 --> 00:17:05.700
of lies so overwhelming that
these people had neither

00:17:05.700 --> 00:17:10.700
the time nor the desire to
turn to novels and stories.

00:17:11.930 --> 00:17:14.150
The assumption here is that in addition

00:17:14.150 --> 00:17:17.610
to being trivial make
believe and a waste of time,

00:17:17.610 --> 00:17:22.540
a classic perennial objection
fiction is a betrayal

00:17:22.540 --> 00:17:25.653
of truth, that it's just a fake.

00:17:26.530 --> 00:17:31.530
But the opposite of truth
is not fiction but falsehood

00:17:33.250 --> 00:17:36.403
and not all fictions are false.

00:17:40.090 --> 00:17:43.603
Section two, a pack of lies.

00:17:48.930 --> 00:17:52.730
Truth is not a tenant
we immediately expect

00:17:52.730 --> 00:17:54.503
to reside in fiction.

00:17:55.440 --> 00:17:58.390
Indeed, we have grown
used to understanding

00:17:58.390 --> 00:18:01.823
the two categories in
opposition to each other.

00:18:02.830 --> 00:18:07.710
Fiction may seem to be
true, it may emulate truth

00:18:07.710 --> 00:18:11.110
and many believe this to be
fiction's greatest imperative

00:18:11.110 --> 00:18:15.690
but it is often defined as an
imaginary construct that in

00:18:15.690 --> 00:18:20.090
the end has little to do
with the pursuit of truth.

00:18:20.090 --> 00:18:22.740
What is expected of made up worlds is

00:18:22.740 --> 00:18:26.600
that they adequately mimic
the real one and short

00:18:26.600 --> 00:18:30.733
of that, that these inventions
be internally consistent.

00:18:32.270 --> 00:18:34.990
Disciplines with an avert claim to truth,

00:18:34.990 --> 00:18:38.330
which include fields as
disparate as the hard sciences,

00:18:38.330 --> 00:18:42.200
the law, journalism, have
always taken good care

00:18:42.200 --> 00:18:47.200
to distance themselves from
fiction, a word they only deploy

00:18:47.290 --> 00:18:48.323
as an insult.

00:18:49.440 --> 00:18:52.377
Yet, I don't believe the
relationship between truth

00:18:52.377 --> 00:18:57.200
and fiction to be one of
rivalry or mutual exclusion.

00:18:57.200 --> 00:19:01.670
I am convinced that
truth resides in some way

00:19:01.670 --> 00:19:03.203
at the heart of fiction.

00:19:06.410 --> 00:19:11.410
In her author's note to
"The Left Hand of Darkness",

00:19:12.840 --> 00:19:16.370
a science fiction novel
set on the remote planet

00:19:16.370 --> 00:19:20.583
of Gethen, Ursula Le
Guin writes and I quote.

00:19:21.947 --> 00:19:26.920
"Fiction writers at least in
their braver moments do desire

00:19:26.920 --> 00:19:31.373
the truth to know it, speak it, serve it.

00:19:32.300 --> 00:19:36.550
But they go about it in a
peculiar and devious way

00:19:36.550 --> 00:19:41.180
which consists in inventing
persons, places and events

00:19:41.180 --> 00:19:44.470
which never did and
never will exist or occur

00:19:44.470 --> 00:19:47.950
and telling about these
fictions in detail and at length

00:19:47.950 --> 00:19:50.740
and with a great deal of emotion.

00:19:50.740 --> 00:19:53.970
And then when they are
done writing down this pack

00:19:53.970 --> 00:19:57.517
of lies they say, there,
that's the truth."

00:19:58.700 --> 00:20:03.210
But what sort of truth is this?

00:20:03.210 --> 00:20:05.370
Excuse me, that was the end of quote.

00:20:05.370 --> 00:20:07.213
But what sort of truth is this?

00:20:08.330 --> 00:20:11.650
I don't think it depends
on how accurately a certain

00:20:11.650 --> 00:20:14.880
historical period is rendered in a novel,

00:20:14.880 --> 00:20:18.640
how precisely a city is
mapped out in its pages

00:20:18.640 --> 00:20:21.653
or how lifelike its characters maybe.

00:20:22.510 --> 00:20:25.920
After all, "The Left Hand
of Darkness" takes place in

00:20:25.920 --> 00:20:29.943
the future in a world
light years away from ours.

00:20:30.890 --> 00:20:34.330
Nor do I think truth
in this context refers

00:20:34.330 --> 00:20:39.330
to how successful the story
is at convincing readers

00:20:40.350 --> 00:20:44.730
to suspend their disbelief no
matter how outlandish the plot

00:20:44.730 --> 00:20:46.583
or the setting happens to be.

00:20:47.610 --> 00:20:50.750
We call such success very similitude

00:20:50.750 --> 00:20:53.633
which means quite literally
resemblance to truth.

00:20:54.500 --> 00:20:59.210
But Le Guin draws a distinction
between the devious way

00:20:59.210 --> 00:21:03.320
in which fiction proceeds by
making things up in detail

00:21:03.320 --> 00:21:07.120
and the final result, this believable,

00:21:07.120 --> 00:21:12.120
lifelike very similar pack
of lies aspires somewhat

00:21:12.300 --> 00:21:17.300
paradoxically the truth
itself not just its likeness.

00:21:19.430 --> 00:21:24.430
In short, truth here has little
to do with a correspondence

00:21:24.900 --> 00:21:29.900
to referential reality, how
closely a story resembles life,

00:21:31.220 --> 00:21:34.570
how much it adheres to empirical facts

00:21:34.570 --> 00:21:38.600
or how accurately it may
capture a historical period

00:21:38.600 --> 00:21:40.143
is not the point.

00:21:41.180 --> 00:21:44.380
Nor are we considering the
epistemological dimension

00:21:44.380 --> 00:21:47.820
of fiction or whether it should
be treated as a collection

00:21:47.820 --> 00:21:50.433
of falsifiable statements.

00:21:51.530 --> 00:21:54.600
The psychological side of
this issue, how faithfully

00:21:54.600 --> 00:21:58.180
the words on the page may
mirror the author's intention

00:21:58.180 --> 00:22:00.623
is equally irrelevant here.

00:22:01.790 --> 00:22:06.790
Lastly, the plausibility of
the story is also an important.

00:22:07.610 --> 00:22:12.230
Just because a realist novel
takes place in Britain during

00:22:12.230 --> 00:22:15.390
the industrial revolution does not mean it

00:22:15.390 --> 00:22:19.720
has a higher truth value than
a science fiction story set

00:22:19.720 --> 00:22:22.653
on an imaginary planet
in the distant future.

00:22:23.760 --> 00:22:27.020
What I think Le Guin is referring to then

00:22:27.020 --> 00:22:30.450
and what I would like to
discuss here fully aware

00:22:30.450 --> 00:22:34.950
of how vague this may
sound is how fiction rather

00:22:34.950 --> 00:22:39.760
than presenting us with
truthful content shows us how

00:22:39.760 --> 00:22:42.363
we experience truth.

00:22:44.430 --> 00:22:46.843
Section three, experience.

00:22:50.130 --> 00:22:55.090
Perhaps fiction aspires to
truth not by capturing the world

00:22:55.090 --> 00:22:59.220
but by presenting the many
ways in which we capture

00:22:59.220 --> 00:23:01.543
the world through experience.

00:23:02.480 --> 00:23:05.150
The question that follows is of course

00:23:05.150 --> 00:23:07.810
what does experience mean?

00:23:07.810 --> 00:23:10.653
And I have two definitions
by two fiction writers.

00:23:14.760 --> 00:23:19.760
In the art of fiction,
1884, Henry James writes,

00:23:21.797 --> 00:23:26.797
"Experience is never limited
and it is never complete.

00:23:27.070 --> 00:23:32.070
It is an immense sensibility,
a kind of huge spider web

00:23:32.380 --> 00:23:36.660
of the finest silicon threads
suspended in the chamber

00:23:36.660 --> 00:23:40.950
of consciousness and catching
every airborne particle

00:23:40.950 --> 00:23:41.857
in its tissue."

00:23:43.950 --> 00:23:45.680
That's James.

00:23:45.680 --> 00:23:50.680
And in "Modern Fiction" of
1919, Virginia Woolf said

00:23:50.710 --> 00:23:54.163
something actually quite
similar and I quote,

00:23:55.497 --> 00:24:00.050
"The mind receives a myriad
impressions - trivial,

00:24:00.050 --> 00:24:03.110
fantastic, evanescent, or engraved with

00:24:03.110 --> 00:24:05.630
the sharpness of steel.

00:24:05.630 --> 00:24:09.350
From all sides they
come an incessant shower

00:24:09.350 --> 00:24:12.750
of innumerable atoms and as they fall,

00:24:12.750 --> 00:24:17.150
as they shape themselves into
the life of Monday or Tuesday

00:24:17.150 --> 00:24:20.917
the accent falls differently from of old."

00:24:25.350 --> 00:24:30.350
A huge spider web of the finest
silicon threads suspended in

00:24:31.130 --> 00:24:35.080
the chamber of
consciousness, writes James.

00:24:35.080 --> 00:24:40.080
The image is as striking as its
implications are remarkable.

00:24:40.500 --> 00:24:45.213
There seems to be a passive
quality to experience here.

00:24:46.570 --> 00:24:50.820
Suspended the net sways
in our consciousness

00:24:50.820 --> 00:24:55.730
almost forgotten and the cobweb
itself immediately conjures

00:24:55.730 --> 00:24:58.953
up images of neglect and abandonment.

00:24:59.970 --> 00:25:03.730
Airborne particles which
I suppose stand here

00:25:03.730 --> 00:25:08.240
for external stimuli simply land on it.

00:25:08.240 --> 00:25:10.363
There is no active reaching out.

00:25:11.290 --> 00:25:14.760
Something similar happens
in Woolf's passage.

00:25:14.760 --> 00:25:19.140
I quote again, "the mind
receives a myriad impressions"

00:25:19.140 --> 00:25:24.140
she writes, "from all sides
they come an" incessant shower

00:25:24.200 --> 00:25:26.507
of innumerable atoms."

00:25:27.720 --> 00:25:31.960
Just like James with his
web, Woolf says we are hit

00:25:31.960 --> 00:25:36.450
with different intensities by
events and impressions some

00:25:36.450 --> 00:25:40.530
of which engrave themselves on the mind.

00:25:40.530 --> 00:25:43.900
There is no active collection
nor is there a selection

00:25:43.900 --> 00:25:47.420
or sifting through these
particles or atoms.

00:25:47.420 --> 00:25:50.730
They simply fall and
shape themselves without

00:25:50.730 --> 00:25:55.730
our intervention into what
we perceive to be our lives.

00:25:55.840 --> 00:25:59.940
Not to the microscopic
scale of the metaphor

00:25:59.940 --> 00:26:03.800
which gives it a
scientific objective slant.

00:26:03.800 --> 00:26:06.380
Experience they both seem to be saying

00:26:06.380 --> 00:26:11.380
is highly granular detailed
down to the atomic level.

00:26:11.860 --> 00:26:15.260
And this is why it is never limited and it

00:26:15.260 --> 00:26:16.793
is never complete.

00:26:18.430 --> 00:26:23.430
Now given this, how should
fiction approach experience?

00:26:24.980 --> 00:26:29.780
Again, James and Woolf
provide similar answers.

00:26:29.780 --> 00:26:31.893
Let's go first with James and I quote.

00:26:34.257 --> 00:26:38.770
"Catching the very note and
trick the strange irregular

00:26:38.770 --> 00:26:43.430
rhythm of life, that is the
attempt whose strenuous force

00:26:43.430 --> 00:26:46.730
keeps fiction upon her feet.

00:26:46.730 --> 00:26:50.120
In proportion as in what
she offers us we see life

00:26:50.120 --> 00:26:54.670
without rearrangement, do
we feel that we are touching

00:26:54.670 --> 00:26:59.670
the truth, in proportion as we
see it with rearrangement do

00:27:00.610 --> 00:27:04.400
we feel that we are being
put off with a substitute,

00:27:04.400 --> 00:27:08.040
a compromise and convention."

00:27:08.040 --> 00:27:12.150
That was Henry James, now Virginia Woolf.

00:27:12.150 --> 00:27:16.320
She writes, "Life is not a series

00:27:16.320 --> 00:27:19.830
of gig lamps symmetrically arranged.

00:27:19.830 --> 00:27:24.830
Life is a luminous halo, a
semi-transparent envelopes

00:27:25.170 --> 00:27:28.460
surrounding us from the
beginning of consciousness

00:27:28.460 --> 00:27:30.070
to the end.

00:27:30.070 --> 00:27:34.640
Is it not the task of the
novelist to convey this varying,

00:27:34.640 --> 00:27:39.640
this unknown and uncircumscribed
spirit, whatever aberration

00:27:39.740 --> 00:27:44.060
or complexity it may display
with as little mixture

00:27:44.060 --> 00:27:47.857
of the alien and external as possible."

00:27:50.290 --> 00:27:55.290
So life is limitless,
complex and uncircumscribed.

00:27:57.690 --> 00:28:02.680
It is inapprehensible in its
every infinitesimal detail.

00:28:02.680 --> 00:28:05.600
Life is not symmetrically arranged

00:28:05.600 --> 00:28:08.930
but has a strange irregular rhythm.

00:28:08.930 --> 00:28:11.610
It doesn't conform to
pre-established norms

00:28:11.610 --> 00:28:16.200
but reinvents itself constantly
according to how the dust,

00:28:16.200 --> 00:28:19.193
the particles, the atoms settles.

00:28:20.100 --> 00:28:24.800
The task of fiction then
is to mimic experience

00:28:24.800 --> 00:28:28.800
and present whatever has
been caught in its web

00:28:28.800 --> 00:28:33.780
without rearrangement,
James even he uses italics.

00:28:33.780 --> 00:28:37.710
Without rearrangement and
with as little mixture

00:28:37.710 --> 00:28:41.343
of the alien and external
as possible, Woolf.

00:28:42.560 --> 00:28:46.250
Just as experience does
not select it's stimuli

00:28:46.250 --> 00:28:50.720
but merely receives them so
the writer should not tamper

00:28:50.720 --> 00:28:55.113
with experience, reorganizing
it or tidying it up.

00:28:56.120 --> 00:28:56.953
Sure.

00:28:58.120 --> 00:29:03.120
I think any random page by
Woolf or James will disprove

00:29:04.300 --> 00:29:06.020
their own precepts.

00:29:06.020 --> 00:29:09.550
Who could seriously believe
that these two wonderful

00:29:09.550 --> 00:29:14.320
control freaks of all
writers did not rearrange

00:29:14.320 --> 00:29:18.220
or mix the alien and
external into what they wrote

00:29:18.220 --> 00:29:21.343
with a certain idea of order in mind.

00:29:22.330 --> 00:29:26.890
I would even claim that the
more they strive to capture

00:29:26.890 --> 00:29:31.810
the strange irregular rhythm
of life the more they focus on

00:29:31.810 --> 00:29:35.350
the semi-transparent
envelope surrounding us,

00:29:35.350 --> 00:29:39.840
the more controlled,
arranged and formerly aware

00:29:39.840 --> 00:29:42.523
the pros become becomes.

00:29:44.140 --> 00:29:47.740
Honoring the opacity of life demands

00:29:47.740 --> 00:29:50.520
great clear sightedness.

00:29:50.520 --> 00:29:55.480
Rendering it's chaotic
complexity requires an equally

00:29:55.480 --> 00:29:58.193
complex sense of order.

00:29:59.150 --> 00:30:02.460
If their pros were insufficient evidence

00:30:02.460 --> 00:30:07.070
the most cursory glance at
Woolf's diary or James' prefaces

00:30:07.070 --> 00:30:08.483
would confirm this.

00:30:10.220 --> 00:30:15.220
We are not perceiving machines,
our eyes are not cameras,

00:30:16.280 --> 00:30:19.573
our ears are not recording devices.

00:30:20.450 --> 00:30:25.230
There is nothing automatic
or passive about perception.

00:30:25.230 --> 00:30:30.230
And unless we become like
Emerson, a transparent eyeball

00:30:31.110 --> 00:30:35.650
that can see all because
it's part we're particle

00:30:35.650 --> 00:30:40.650
of God we need to accept that
perception is rearrangement

00:30:42.120 --> 00:30:44.970
and that multiple processes of selection

00:30:44.970 --> 00:30:49.970
and readjustment are at play
as we experience the world.

00:30:51.420 --> 00:30:56.320
Far from dismissing or concealing
them literature captures

00:30:56.320 --> 00:30:59.343
and even reveals these processes.

00:31:00.260 --> 00:31:05.260
It is precisely because it
rearranges and intervenes

00:31:05.380 --> 00:31:10.380
from outside that fiction
imitates the way we perceive life

00:31:10.930 --> 00:31:13.500
which is always through some sort

00:31:13.500 --> 00:31:18.000
of substitute, compromise and convention.

00:31:18.000 --> 00:31:21.020
And these three terms which James dreaded

00:31:21.020 --> 00:31:25.710
so much define not only
literary forms and genres

00:31:25.710 --> 00:31:28.423
but also language itself.

00:31:29.280 --> 00:31:32.223
Substitute, compromise, convention.

00:31:34.720 --> 00:31:39.720
Section four, the title is the field,

00:31:40.050 --> 00:31:42.023
the window, the watcher.

00:31:43.930 --> 00:31:48.170
In 1908, almost 25 years
after the publication

00:31:48.170 --> 00:31:51.360
of "The Art of Fiction"
James wrote the preface

00:31:51.360 --> 00:31:55.063
to the New York edition
of the portrait of a lady.

00:31:55.920 --> 00:31:59.810
Here, the chamber of
consciousness has expanded

00:31:59.810 --> 00:32:03.120
to become the famous house
of fiction from which

00:32:03.120 --> 00:32:07.253
the writer gets a necessarily
partial view of life.

00:32:08.860 --> 00:32:12.100
Gone from this house
are the cobwebs hanging

00:32:12.100 --> 00:32:13.793
in his earlier essay.

00:32:14.660 --> 00:32:18.690
No longer a passive figure,
the writer now needs

00:32:18.690 --> 00:32:22.360
to peers holes in the wall
in order to get a view

00:32:22.360 --> 00:32:24.950
of the world beyond.

00:32:24.950 --> 00:32:29.950
There are, "A million possible
windows that could open up

00:32:30.440 --> 00:32:35.053
to an incalculability
of range," says James.

00:32:36.180 --> 00:32:39.440
The particular aperture the
writer creates will determine

00:32:39.440 --> 00:32:42.270
which portion of that unlimited experience

00:32:42.270 --> 00:32:44.393
can be perceived and narrated.

00:32:45.530 --> 00:32:47.033
And I quote again from James.

00:32:48.397 --> 00:32:52.150
"The spreading field, the human scene,

00:32:52.150 --> 00:32:55.030
is the choice of subject.

00:32:55.030 --> 00:32:59.320
The pierced aperture, either
broad or balconied or slit-like

00:32:59.320 --> 00:33:04.320
and low-browed is the literary
form but they are singly

00:33:05.310 --> 00:33:09.410
or together as nothing
without the posted presence

00:33:09.410 --> 00:33:14.410
of the watcher without in
other words, the consciousness

00:33:14.660 --> 00:33:15.823
of the artist.

00:33:17.410 --> 00:33:18.623
That was Henry James.

00:33:19.490 --> 00:33:24.490
The resident after all a
need to rearrange and frame

00:33:25.430 --> 00:33:28.853
the strange irregular rhythm of life.

00:33:29.790 --> 00:33:33.440
Form is what comes
between the thing watched

00:33:33.440 --> 00:33:34.960
and the watcher.

00:33:34.960 --> 00:33:39.070
Conditioning what we can
see and how we see it.

00:33:39.070 --> 00:33:41.640
Expanding James is similarly, let's say

00:33:41.640 --> 00:33:44.253
there are three types of stories.

00:33:45.120 --> 00:33:48.210
Those that focus on the
spreading field outside the house

00:33:48.210 --> 00:33:52.660
of fiction, those that are
concerned with the design

00:33:52.660 --> 00:33:57.660
of the window framing that
view and those that focus on

00:33:57.770 --> 00:34:01.333
the watcher or the
consciousness of the artist.

00:34:02.530 --> 00:34:05.320
Of course this is schematic.

00:34:05.320 --> 00:34:09.890
The categories overlap and good
fiction usually has element

00:34:09.890 --> 00:34:14.593
of all three but perhaps you'll
bear with me and indulge me.

00:34:16.600 --> 00:34:21.600
An emblematic novel in the
first group is "The Life

00:34:21.890 --> 00:34:25.300
and Strange Surprising
Adventures of Robinson Crusoe,

00:34:25.300 --> 00:34:28.930
of York, Mariner" written by himself.

00:34:28.930 --> 00:34:33.160
Of course, the last part of
the title "Written by Himself"

00:34:33.160 --> 00:34:35.770
already tells us where this is going and

00:34:35.770 --> 00:34:39.710
the preface pen by an editor confirms it.

00:34:39.710 --> 00:34:43.480
And I quote from this preface
it says, "the editor believes

00:34:43.480 --> 00:34:47.900
the thing to be just
history of fact, neither

00:34:47.900 --> 00:34:51.237
is there any appearance of fiction in it."

00:34:53.200 --> 00:34:58.200
So whether it succeeds or not
the novel stakes its claim

00:34:58.770 --> 00:35:03.180
on its truthfulness and
denies its fictional quality.

00:35:03.180 --> 00:35:04.513
This is not fiction.

00:35:06.340 --> 00:35:09.720
Narratives like this
compel us through a variety

00:35:09.720 --> 00:35:14.070
of rhetorical means to
forget they are fictive.

00:35:14.070 --> 00:35:17.580
They present us with a
world and ask us to pretend

00:35:17.580 --> 00:35:20.193
that we take it for the real thing.

00:35:21.250 --> 00:35:26.250
This group includes I think
most modern western fiction.

00:35:27.270 --> 00:35:30.040
It was consolidated during
the rise of realism in

00:35:30.040 --> 00:35:34.590
the 19th Century and
still holds sway today.

00:35:34.590 --> 00:35:38.900
All those life-like stories
that regardless of genre

00:35:38.900 --> 00:35:43.090
or how fanciful their setting
may be rely on readers

00:35:43.090 --> 00:35:47.970
suspension of disbelief on
their accepting for a moment

00:35:47.970 --> 00:35:50.713
that they're being
presented with the actual.

00:35:51.930 --> 00:35:55.650
Even speculative novels like
Le Guin's may fall under

00:35:55.650 --> 00:35:57.173
this category by the way.

00:35:58.220 --> 00:36:02.820
So this is fiction that
wishes we could forget

00:36:02.820 --> 00:36:07.030
that we're looking through
a framed pane of glass

00:36:07.030 --> 00:36:12.030
and believe rather that we
are on the spreading field.

00:36:14.070 --> 00:36:18.330
The second group, literature
that shows an avert concern

00:36:18.330 --> 00:36:21.313
for the window has a strange history.

00:36:22.350 --> 00:36:25.330
Consider the authors apology for this book

00:36:25.330 --> 00:36:29.240
that precedes "The Pilgrim's
Progress" where John Bunyan

00:36:29.240 --> 00:36:32.210
openly announces that the following pages

00:36:32.210 --> 00:36:37.210
are a fable mere fancies which
rather than imitating life

00:36:38.940 --> 00:36:43.590
are in the similitude of a
dream or a factory's preface

00:36:43.590 --> 00:36:47.750
to vanity fair titled
before the curtain in which

00:36:47.750 --> 00:36:51.640
the author introduces
himself as the manager

00:36:51.640 --> 00:36:53.223
of the performance.

00:36:54.070 --> 00:36:58.690
On the stage, they're not
even actors but puppets,

00:36:58.690 --> 00:37:00.543
dolls and figures.

00:37:01.410 --> 00:37:03.530
These are three words from the preface.

00:37:03.530 --> 00:37:06.970
The manager's last gesture
in the preface is to bow

00:37:06.970 --> 00:37:11.970
to his patrons and rise the
curtain to reveal the stage.

00:37:12.120 --> 00:37:15.725
This curtain it's the
same one that James draws

00:37:15.725 --> 00:37:18.170
to look out his window.

00:37:18.170 --> 00:37:22.960
The window that stands let's
remember for literal form.

00:37:22.960 --> 00:37:26.270
This brings me to the strange evolution

00:37:26.270 --> 00:37:29.260
of fiction in this category.

00:37:29.260 --> 00:37:34.150
I believe the apologetic
drive which can be one

00:37:34.150 --> 00:37:37.250
with impulse to reduce
fiction to a mere allegory

00:37:37.250 --> 00:37:42.250
or a fable that never asks to
be taken at face value became

00:37:42.500 --> 00:37:46.350
the self-referential
gesture that defines so much

00:37:46.350 --> 00:37:50.423
of what we may hastily
call experimental fiction.

00:37:51.320 --> 00:37:55.160
I place in this group all
stories that draw attention

00:37:55.160 --> 00:37:58.720
to the fact that they
are verbal fabrications.

00:37:58.720 --> 00:38:03.720
Narratives that rather than
trying to pass for the truth

00:38:03.810 --> 00:38:07.270
and now in different ways
their fictional status

00:38:07.270 --> 00:38:11.010
and reflect on their formal configuration.

00:38:11.010 --> 00:38:14.360
This includes all event called
literature that works on

00:38:14.360 --> 00:38:19.160
the premise of alienation,
estrangement or distancing,

00:38:19.160 --> 00:38:23.423
showing and drawing attention
to the artifice of it all.

00:38:24.840 --> 00:38:28.260
Closer to our present time
what is known as metafiction

00:38:28.260 --> 00:38:31.530
and other self-conscious
narratives may fall under

00:38:31.530 --> 00:38:33.220
this category as well.

00:38:33.220 --> 00:38:37.233
In short, all those novel
that Elizabeth Hardwick put

00:38:38.410 --> 00:38:42.040
it in reflection on fiction show a degree

00:38:42.040 --> 00:38:44.193
of panic about the form.

00:38:46.500 --> 00:38:50.900
And finally, we have
the fiction focused on

00:38:50.900 --> 00:38:55.900
the posted presence of the
watcher or in other words

00:38:55.960 --> 00:38:58.403
the consciousness of the artist.

00:38:59.940 --> 00:39:03.530
With a slight of hand in other words

00:39:03.530 --> 00:39:06.510
is what James slips in there.

00:39:06.510 --> 00:39:10.680
James conflates two very different worlds.

00:39:10.680 --> 00:39:15.680
On the one hand, the watcher
is posted in the house

00:39:16.290 --> 00:39:20.010
of fiction looking out
the window which makes

00:39:20.010 --> 00:39:23.020
the watcher a textual being just like

00:39:23.020 --> 00:39:24.773
the characters on the lawn.

00:39:26.050 --> 00:39:29.840
On the other, the consciousness
of the artist refers

00:39:29.840 --> 00:39:33.470
to someone from an
altogether different plane,

00:39:33.470 --> 00:39:37.813
someone this side of the
book like the readers.

00:39:38.950 --> 00:39:43.950
It is safe I think to equate
the watcher with a narrator

00:39:44.070 --> 00:39:47.420
while the consciousness
of the artist is closer

00:39:47.420 --> 00:39:50.470
to the author without of
course fully overlapping

00:39:50.470 --> 00:39:53.733
with this extra literary presence.

00:39:55.670 --> 00:40:00.640
Nothing in fiction neither
the scene nor the frame exists

00:40:00.640 --> 00:40:05.360
without this perceiving
watcher, says James.

00:40:05.360 --> 00:40:10.240
But this hybrid undecisive
ever mutating being

00:40:11.330 --> 00:40:15.780
it's alternatively and
often simultaneously inside

00:40:15.780 --> 00:40:18.360
and outside the text.

00:40:18.360 --> 00:40:20.910
It's view is limited
by point of view which

00:40:20.910 --> 00:40:24.090
is very much an internal
construction of the story

00:40:24.090 --> 00:40:28.010
but it's also informed by the
consciousness of the writer

00:40:28.010 --> 00:40:32.270
which isn't quite a literary occurrence.

00:40:32.270 --> 00:40:36.790
This presence inhabits a liminal zone.

00:40:36.790 --> 00:40:40.620
It can jump out the window
and join the party out on

00:40:40.620 --> 00:40:44.710
the lawn as do all the
first person narrators

00:40:44.710 --> 00:40:49.710
but it can also turn its back
to the window and face us.

00:40:53.340 --> 00:40:57.070
I have always been drawn
to stories that revolve

00:40:57.070 --> 00:41:00.190
around this presence
because they often try

00:41:00.190 --> 00:41:04.520
to complicate inherited notions
of what literature may be

00:41:05.400 --> 00:41:07.170
and they do so through the process

00:41:07.170 --> 00:41:09.913
of unfolding themselves as literature.

00:41:10.830 --> 00:41:13.520
The classic example would
be the nesting dolls

00:41:13.520 --> 00:41:16.010
in "Donkey Xote" in which the distinctions

00:41:16.010 --> 00:41:21.010
among author, narrator, character
and reader are in crisis.

00:41:21.130 --> 00:41:24.190
Which means the distinction
between the inside

00:41:24.190 --> 00:41:28.993
and the outside of the house
of fiction is also unclear.

00:41:30.160 --> 00:41:33.380
Here are some of my favorite texts

00:41:33.380 --> 00:41:36.460
that question these boundaries.

00:41:36.460 --> 00:41:39.340
Machado de Assis does it
in "The Posthumous Memoirs

00:41:39.340 --> 00:41:42.110
of Bras Cubas" in which
his dead character is also

00:41:42.110 --> 00:41:45.500
the narrator who promises
to reach out of the book

00:41:45.500 --> 00:41:48.950
and flick his reader in the
head if he doesn't like it.

00:41:48.950 --> 00:41:50.730
Robert Walser does it in "The robber"

00:41:50.730 --> 00:41:53.570
with his authorial interventions.

00:41:53.570 --> 00:41:55.660
Macedonio Fernandez does it in "The Museum

00:41:55.660 --> 00:41:59.020
of Eterna's Novel" a novel
made up of four words

00:41:59.020 --> 00:42:00.410
to that novel.

00:42:00.410 --> 00:42:03.500
Jorge Luis Borges does
it in several stories

00:42:03.500 --> 00:42:06.610
by directly addressing and
involving the reader in

00:42:06.610 --> 00:42:09.910
the plot by silently quoting himself

00:42:09.910 --> 00:42:14.210
and by making several
hitchcock-like cameos.

00:42:14.210 --> 00:42:17.720
Clarice Lispector does it in
"The Hour of the Star" in which

00:42:17.720 --> 00:42:21.133
the narrator is a character
writing the story we're reading.

00:42:22.190 --> 00:42:26.000
Muriel Spark does it in "The
Comforters", her first novel

00:42:26.000 --> 00:42:30.000
in which the protagonist
literally hears the author type

00:42:30.000 --> 00:42:32.310
her thoughts as she is thinking them

00:42:32.310 --> 00:42:34.410
and we are reading them.

00:42:34.410 --> 00:42:39.410
Samuel Beckett does it
through the pronominal shifts

00:42:41.340 --> 00:42:46.280
in "Company", David Markson
does it masterfully over

00:42:46.280 --> 00:42:48.850
and over again in his last four novels

00:42:48.850 --> 00:42:52.720
where he appears pitched
against the entire Western-canon

00:42:52.720 --> 00:42:57.373
under the name of reader,
protagonist, novelist or author.

00:42:59.840 --> 00:43:04.840
This texts fold upon
themselves and in a mobius-like

00:43:05.620 --> 00:43:10.573
contortion become their
own referential reality.

00:43:12.360 --> 00:43:16.573
Gertrude Stein could not
have put it more clearly.

00:43:19.624 --> 00:43:21.963
And I quote, "the more a novel
is a novel, the more a play

00:43:24.930 --> 00:43:28.360
is a play, the more a
writing is a writing,

00:43:28.360 --> 00:43:32.550
the more no outside is
outside, outside is inside,

00:43:32.550 --> 00:43:34.287
inside is inside."

00:43:37.440 --> 00:43:42.420
I distrust taxonomies and
grids of any sort applied

00:43:42.420 --> 00:43:45.830
to literature but with this hesitating

00:43:45.830 --> 00:43:48.680
and I'm sure a questionable
classification I hope

00:43:48.680 --> 00:43:50.820
to show how fiction can have different

00:43:50.820 --> 00:43:53.233
dispositions toward truth.

00:43:54.080 --> 00:43:57.610
Fiction concerned with the
spreading field is true

00:43:57.610 --> 00:44:01.820
because it mimics certain
unquestioning ways in which

00:44:01.820 --> 00:44:03.883
we experience reality.

00:44:04.960 --> 00:44:09.700
Fiction concerned with the
peers to aperture is true

00:44:09.700 --> 00:44:13.980
because it's honest about
its own artificial status

00:44:13.980 --> 00:44:17.120
and it makes us consider the
extent to which reality may

00:44:17.120 --> 00:44:20.350
also be a framed narrative.

00:44:20.350 --> 00:44:24.450
And fiction concerned
with the watcher is true

00:44:24.450 --> 00:44:29.450
because it understands the
ambiguity and instability of all

00:44:30.290 --> 00:44:35.290
the terms involved here mainly
reality, fiction and frame.

00:44:36.790 --> 00:44:41.160
In the end, these are
all ways of experiencing

00:44:41.160 --> 00:44:43.780
our relationship to the world which

00:44:43.780 --> 00:44:48.780
at times can be immediate
and wholly immersive

00:44:48.990 --> 00:44:53.990
at times painfully and
self-consciously distant.

00:44:56.490 --> 00:45:01.483
Five and last, the emotion itself.

00:45:05.010 --> 00:45:10.010
In October 1926, The Yale
Review published an essay

00:45:12.320 --> 00:45:16.440
by Virginia Woolf titled "How
Should One Read a Book?" Which

00:45:16.440 --> 00:45:19.853
is a wonderful essay
that we should all read.

00:45:21.670 --> 00:45:25.670
A few pages into her piece she explains

00:45:25.670 --> 00:45:29.890
the fiction writers work and
I'll quote here a little bit

00:45:29.890 --> 00:45:31.763
at length but it's a beautiful text.

00:45:33.860 --> 00:45:37.960
Woolf rights, "Perhaps the
quickest way to understand

00:45:37.960 --> 00:45:42.230
the elements of what a novelist
is doing it's not to read

00:45:42.230 --> 00:45:46.980
but to write, to make your own
experiment with the dangers

00:45:46.980 --> 00:45:50.080
and difficulties of words.

00:45:50.080 --> 00:45:53.520
Recall then some event
that has left a distinct

00:45:53.520 --> 00:45:55.610
impression on you.

00:45:55.610 --> 00:45:57.700
How at the corner of the street perhaps

00:45:57.700 --> 00:46:01.550
you pass two people talking, a tree shook,

00:46:01.550 --> 00:46:04.250
an electric light danced.

00:46:04.250 --> 00:46:08.150
The tone of the talk was
comic but also tragic.

00:46:08.150 --> 00:46:11.870
A whole vision, an entire
conception seem contained

00:46:11.870 --> 00:46:12.933
in that moment.

00:46:14.000 --> 00:46:17.030
But when you attempt to
reconstruct it in words

00:46:17.030 --> 00:46:20.860
you'll find that it breaks
into a thousand conflicting

00:46:20.860 --> 00:46:25.860
impressions, some must be
subdued, others emphasized.

00:46:26.640 --> 00:46:31.000
In the process you will
lose probably all grasp upon

00:46:31.000 --> 00:46:33.820
the emotion itself.

00:46:33.820 --> 00:46:37.330
Then turn from your
blurred and littered pages

00:46:37.330 --> 00:46:40.390
to the opening pages
of some great novelist,

00:46:40.390 --> 00:46:43.960
Defoe, Jane Austen, Hardy.

00:46:43.960 --> 00:46:48.797
Now, you will be better able
to appreciate their mastery."

00:46:49.714 --> 00:46:51.493
That's the end of Woolf.

00:46:53.600 --> 00:46:58.350
If James' view of the novelists
task changed over time

00:46:58.350 --> 00:47:01.190
so did Woolf's during the seven years

00:47:01.190 --> 00:47:05.340
that separate modern fiction,
the first text I quoted

00:47:05.340 --> 00:47:07.050
from this text.

00:47:07.050 --> 00:47:12.050
Here like there, impressions
are the point of departure.

00:47:12.800 --> 00:47:17.030
Here like there, we are
confronted with the multifaceted

00:47:17.030 --> 00:47:20.800
and uncontainable nature of experience.

00:47:20.800 --> 00:47:24.390
And by the way, note the unstable light

00:47:24.390 --> 00:47:26.670
illuminating both passages.

00:47:26.670 --> 00:47:29.310
A semi-transparent halo in the first now,

00:47:29.310 --> 00:47:31.070
a flickering bulb here.

00:47:31.070 --> 00:47:33.520
As if to show us there is something opaque

00:47:33.520 --> 00:47:35.973
and wavering about perception.

00:47:37.790 --> 00:47:41.590
But instead of the
almost mystical passivity

00:47:41.590 --> 00:47:44.420
that Woolf encouraged in modern fiction,

00:47:44.420 --> 00:47:47.010
in this essay she acknowledges the need

00:47:47.010 --> 00:47:51.860
to subdue some impressions
while emphasizing others.

00:47:51.860 --> 00:47:55.040
Blurriness unless deliberately crafted

00:47:55.040 --> 00:47:57.653
is not a desired outcome.

00:47:58.850 --> 00:48:03.080
Without some sort of order
the myriad impressions

00:48:03.080 --> 00:48:07.770
that fall on the mind are
mere litter on the page.

00:48:07.770 --> 00:48:11.360
Crucially though, form
should be at the service

00:48:11.360 --> 00:48:15.700
of keeping one's grasp
upon the emotion itself

00:48:15.700 --> 00:48:18.203
and this brings me to my final thoughts.

00:48:19.667 --> 00:48:24.087
To recapitulate fiction that
pack of lies is in pursuit

00:48:25.080 --> 00:48:28.000
of its own form of truth.

00:48:28.000 --> 00:48:31.270
I know this truth has
little to do with accurately

00:48:31.270 --> 00:48:34.030
describing referential reality.

00:48:34.030 --> 00:48:37.990
I suspect it may be related
to the attempt at presenting

00:48:37.990 --> 00:48:41.870
the many ways in which
we experience the world.

00:48:41.870 --> 00:48:45.760
I believe this attempt
to be mediated by form,

00:48:45.760 --> 00:48:49.710
the individual choices and
inherited configurations

00:48:49.710 --> 00:48:52.760
by which we rearrange our perceptions.

00:48:52.760 --> 00:48:56.980
I also think that literature
is disposition toward form

00:48:56.980 --> 00:49:01.800
the ways in which it
conceals, reveals and reflects

00:49:01.800 --> 00:49:06.000
on its artificial nature
defines its distinctive

00:49:06.000 --> 00:49:07.773
relationship to truth.

00:49:08.770 --> 00:49:13.060
But this definition seems insufficient.

00:49:13.060 --> 00:49:18.060
It reduces fiction to more
or less self-aware reports

00:49:18.260 --> 00:49:20.573
on non-existent worlds.

00:49:21.590 --> 00:49:25.960
These reports may mimic with
different degrees of success

00:49:25.960 --> 00:49:29.110
the ways in which we
relate to the actual world,

00:49:29.110 --> 00:49:33.730
still fiction does more than this.

00:49:33.730 --> 00:49:38.730
It not only mimics experience,
it is itself an experience.

00:49:42.650 --> 00:49:44.440
Let's return to Woolf.

00:49:44.440 --> 00:49:46.950
She notes that a
hypothetical writer trying

00:49:46.950 --> 00:49:51.290
to capture experience will
likely lose all grasp upon

00:49:51.290 --> 00:49:53.420
the emotion itself.

00:49:53.420 --> 00:49:55.910
Grasping an emotion, this seems to be

00:49:55.910 --> 00:49:58.210
fiction's main purpose.

00:49:58.210 --> 00:50:01.180
The emotion is what
needs to be conjured up

00:50:01.180 --> 00:50:03.223
and preserved in the text.

00:50:04.100 --> 00:50:08.270
It is this to which all
other elements including form

00:50:08.270 --> 00:50:12.730
are subordinated because
form as George Eliot noted

00:50:12.730 --> 00:50:17.730
in an 1868 essay is, "Not
only determined by emotion

00:50:18.100 --> 00:50:20.747
but intended to express it."

00:50:23.320 --> 00:50:27.310
I ought to underscore
at once that I am not,

00:50:27.310 --> 00:50:30.410
most emphatically not speaking here about

00:50:30.410 --> 00:50:34.840
the many vicarious
sentiments a story may seek

00:50:34.840 --> 00:50:36.923
to exciten us as we read.

00:50:37.920 --> 00:50:41.470
It's not about feeling
Captain Ahab's wrath,

00:50:41.470 --> 00:50:45.810
Emma Bovary's desire or
Hans Cousto's malaise

00:50:45.810 --> 00:50:48.623
as if these emotions were our own.

00:50:49.950 --> 00:50:53.390
I'm referring rather to the
general feeling that fiction

00:50:53.390 --> 00:50:58.390
when successful stirs in us
regardless of specific content.

00:50:59.870 --> 00:51:03.117
In his lecture in Dickens,
Nabokov says and I quote,

00:51:03.117 --> 00:51:05.630
"Although we read with our minds, the seat

00:51:05.630 --> 00:51:09.000
of artistic delight is
between the shoulder blades.

00:51:09.000 --> 00:51:11.540
That little shiver
behind is quite certainly

00:51:11.540 --> 00:51:15.120
the highest form of emotion that humanity

00:51:15.120 --> 00:51:19.257
has attained when evolving
pure art and pure science."

00:51:22.050 --> 00:51:26.170
I must confess I've never
felt that shiver down my spine

00:51:26.170 --> 00:51:29.230
to me it's more like a body-wide fist but

00:51:30.570 --> 00:51:35.350
the point still stands and I agree.

00:51:35.350 --> 00:51:38.640
Artistic delight is one
of the highest forms

00:51:38.640 --> 00:51:41.050
of emotion we can experience.

00:51:41.050 --> 00:51:46.050
And this I think is the
emotion Woolf is referring to

00:51:46.210 --> 00:51:50.900
and without it fiction,
however formerly sophisticated

00:51:50.900 --> 00:51:54.630
becomes simply a pack of lies.

00:51:54.630 --> 00:51:59.630
In that same essay George
Eliot asks and I quote,

00:52:00.007 --> 00:52:03.480
"What is fiction other than an arrangement

00:52:03.480 --> 00:52:07.280
of events or faint
correspondences according

00:52:07.280 --> 00:52:09.707
to a predominant feeling."

00:52:12.020 --> 00:52:15.370
There is so much to love about this pithy

00:52:15.370 --> 00:52:17.310
definition of fiction.

00:52:17.310 --> 00:52:21.260
The main one for me is
the marriage between form

00:52:21.260 --> 00:52:25.270
and feeling which again, I take
to be an aesthetic emotion.

00:52:25.270 --> 00:52:29.710
One that is free from any
sort of correspondence with

00:52:29.710 --> 00:52:32.170
the referential world.

00:52:32.170 --> 00:52:36.280
For it has nothing to do with
imitating any passions in

00:52:36.280 --> 00:52:38.080
our emotional palette.

00:52:38.080 --> 00:52:42.580
A rush of courage or a pang
of guilt we may recognize in

00:52:42.580 --> 00:52:44.483
this or that character.

00:52:45.760 --> 00:52:49.850
The feeling of artistic
delight is conjured up by

00:52:49.850 --> 00:52:54.850
the text itself and it's not
anchored to anything external.

00:52:56.270 --> 00:53:00.690
Furthermore, it cannot be
found outside the text.

00:53:00.690 --> 00:53:05.690
It is a literary event
exclusively because this

00:53:07.070 --> 00:53:10.750
particular experience
has one defining quality.

00:53:10.750 --> 00:53:15.750
Since literature's prime
material is language it follows

00:53:16.230 --> 00:53:21.230
that this aesthetic emotion
will be not only a sensory event

00:53:21.520 --> 00:53:26.110
as the delight we can find
in pure color or impure sound

00:53:26.110 --> 00:53:28.943
but also a semantic one.

00:53:30.090 --> 00:53:33.850
Fiction is not a collection of falsehood

00:53:33.850 --> 00:53:36.640
or irrelevant fabrications.

00:53:36.640 --> 00:53:41.640
It is rather a certain kind
of narrative that shows us

00:53:41.720 --> 00:53:46.290
the many ways in which we
experience life whether it wishes

00:53:46.290 --> 00:53:50.163
to accurately address
referential reality or not.

00:53:51.000 --> 00:53:56.000
And it does so while adding
an experience to that life.

00:53:57.530 --> 00:54:01.890
What happens to us this
feeling of plenitude when

00:54:01.890 --> 00:54:06.890
we read fiction takes place
at the intersection of meaning

00:54:07.000 --> 00:54:11.163
and emotion and it is
always mediated by form.

00:54:12.300 --> 00:54:15.730
The physical enjoyment
of language in its visual

00:54:15.730 --> 00:54:18.220
and oral dimensions.

00:54:18.220 --> 00:54:21.700
The wonder of seeing
something come into being

00:54:21.700 --> 00:54:26.700
as it takes shape in a sentence,
the many avocations a word

00:54:27.220 --> 00:54:32.220
or paragraph may conjure up,
the miracle of time expanding

00:54:32.940 --> 00:54:37.940
and contracting on the page,
the love a certain verbal

00:54:37.940 --> 00:54:40.090
creature may inspire.

00:54:40.090 --> 00:54:43.790
These are some of the ways
in which we experience

00:54:43.790 --> 00:54:48.790
this intensity which shifts
fiction away from the imperative

00:54:48.850 --> 00:54:52.930
of correspondence with
the empirical world.

00:54:52.930 --> 00:54:57.230
And it is this emotion
that brings friction closer

00:54:57.230 --> 00:55:02.230
to truth while freeing it
from its referential anxiety

00:55:02.780 --> 00:55:07.780
showing us how much beauty
there can be in meaning.

00:55:08.500 --> 00:55:09.400
Thank you so much.

00:55:12.330 --> 00:55:15.700
- Thank you so much Hernan,
that was really wonderful.

00:55:15.700 --> 00:55:18.420
So many ideas to think
about and unpack here

00:55:18.420 --> 00:55:22.360
and sadly I know we won't get
to talk about all of them.

00:55:22.360 --> 00:55:27.010
I want to remind our audience
that they can ask questions in

00:55:27.010 --> 00:55:29.890
the Q and A even if we
don't get to talk about all

00:55:29.890 --> 00:55:32.580
our questions today I hope
that you'll be able to read

00:55:32.580 --> 00:55:34.600
the piece in the review and
that will be another way

00:55:34.600 --> 00:55:37.070
to engage with this but I
thought I would start us off

00:55:37.070 --> 00:55:38.833
and then turn to our audience.

00:55:40.040 --> 00:55:41.730
I'm not sure where I wanna
start because there's

00:55:41.730 --> 00:55:44.330
so many things I wanted to
ask about but I think I'd like

00:55:44.330 --> 00:55:48.190
to start by returning to
what you just talked about

00:55:48.190 --> 00:55:50.750
about this feeling of
plenitude when we read fiction

00:55:50.750 --> 00:55:53.820
deriving from as you put
the intersection of meaning

00:55:53.820 --> 00:55:56.780
and emotion and always mediated by form.

00:55:56.780 --> 00:55:58.990
And I wonder if we could
pivot to talk a little bit

00:55:58.990 --> 00:56:03.080
about writing fiction and how that sense

00:56:03.080 --> 00:56:05.420
of plenitude comes into writing and how

00:56:05.420 --> 00:56:09.167
you free yourself from
the referential adherence

00:56:13.130 --> 00:56:14.550
that you talked about.

00:56:14.550 --> 00:56:17.150
And that might take us
back to the very beginning

00:56:17.150 --> 00:56:19.700
of the talk where you
talked about the difference

00:56:19.700 --> 00:56:23.080
between reading and research.

00:56:23.080 --> 00:56:26.910
And perhaps you could unpack
this a little bit more from

00:56:26.910 --> 00:56:28.890
the writer's point of view.

00:56:29.760 --> 00:56:32.170
- Well, thank you and I'm sorry I see

00:56:32.170 --> 00:56:34.190
that I took a little
longer than anticipated

00:56:34.190 --> 00:56:35.390
so I apologize for that.

00:56:37.910 --> 00:56:40.620
How to escape the referential
anxiety during the process

00:56:40.620 --> 00:56:45.620
of writing I think the
answer is contained someplace

00:56:46.950 --> 00:56:49.410
within this talk.

00:56:49.410 --> 00:56:52.370
I would like to say nothing
is as tidy and you're a writer

00:56:52.370 --> 00:56:53.670
so you know this.

00:56:53.670 --> 00:56:57.510
Nothing is as tidy as it comes
off in this presentation.

00:56:57.510 --> 00:57:02.253
Everything is much more
it's a mess, it's whimsical,

00:57:03.460 --> 00:57:06.683
it's not a controlled
environment at least for me,

00:57:08.160 --> 00:57:10.743
maybe some people are more methodic.

00:57:13.216 --> 00:57:17.317
But I would say that for
me it's always putting

00:57:18.560 --> 00:57:23.050
at the center the
emotional aspect of writing

00:57:23.050 --> 00:57:25.910
and I think that's the
beautiful thing about fiction

00:57:25.910 --> 00:57:28.360
that it's a highly
intellectual endeavor but

00:57:28.360 --> 00:57:32.173
at the same time it has to
be emotionally stirring.

00:57:33.200 --> 00:57:36.170
That's true for all literature
and not just fiction,

00:57:36.170 --> 00:57:38.770
it applies obviously to
poetry as well for instance.

00:57:39.870 --> 00:57:44.767
So that's sort of my
inner calibrating device.

00:57:47.440 --> 00:57:52.440
If I'm leaning toward one
side or the other I feel that

00:57:55.160 --> 00:57:57.940
if you become too focused
on the intellectual side on

00:57:57.940 --> 00:58:02.940
the formal side, you run
the risk of just building a

00:58:03.990 --> 00:58:07.090
very beautiful but cold cathedral that

00:58:07.090 --> 00:58:10.150
is completely uninhabited.

00:58:10.150 --> 00:58:13.660
But on the other hand, if you
overplay the emotional side,

00:58:13.660 --> 00:58:18.660
I mean, nobody's interested
in like feelings, right?

00:58:19.200 --> 00:58:24.200
That's unmediated feelings
are a little yucky so yeah.

00:58:25.967 --> 00:58:28.890
- Or at least as I tell
my students you could have

00:58:28.890 --> 00:58:30.770
the biggest feelings
in the world and sadly

00:58:30.770 --> 00:58:34.590
they do not translate to the
biggest productions, right?

00:58:34.590 --> 00:58:35.490
- Yes.
- (Indistinct).

00:58:37.890 --> 00:58:40.760
You talked in some interviews
I read about this distinction

00:58:40.760 --> 00:58:45.023
between reading and research
and I thought it was so astute.

00:58:46.000 --> 00:58:48.400
I remember having a moment writing a poem

00:58:48.400 --> 00:58:51.450
where I had done a lot of
research into 19th Century books

00:58:51.450 --> 00:58:55.190
where I had kind of put in some
detail about pigskin vellum

00:58:55.190 --> 00:58:57.420
and I thought no, no, this
speaker of this pump would not

00:58:57.420 --> 00:59:01.610
be thinking about her book this
way, it would just be right.

00:59:01.610 --> 00:59:04.470
So part of what you seem to
get at in this distinction

00:59:04.470 --> 00:59:07.070
between reading and research
that I think applies back

00:59:07.070 --> 00:59:10.380
to what you're talking
about here, is the idea

00:59:10.380 --> 00:59:13.960
that it's not verisimilitude
necessarily that you're looking

00:59:13.960 --> 00:59:18.960
for it's this more complex
dimensional world-building.

00:59:21.750 --> 00:59:24.380
I guess sort of combination
of world and effect building

00:59:24.380 --> 00:59:26.490
but I was wondering what
that actually looks like in

00:59:26.490 --> 00:59:30.490
the distance is set in a
time that is not our time.

00:59:30.490 --> 00:59:33.610
So how do you distinguish
you are in a sense

00:59:33.610 --> 00:59:34.870
doing research, right?

00:59:34.870 --> 00:59:37.850
But clearly there's a distinction for you.

00:59:37.850 --> 00:59:41.100
How do you put that into
practice and what does it mean?

00:59:41.100 --> 00:59:44.770
- Well, there is a distinction for me

00:59:44.770 --> 00:59:49.723
and I hope I'm not doing
research as defined a moment ago.

00:59:50.930 --> 00:59:54.610
I have to say also I was
trained as an academic

00:59:54.610 --> 00:59:59.610
so I do have like a lot of
experience with archival work

01:00:01.140 --> 01:00:01.990
and library work.

01:00:01.990 --> 01:00:03.710
I'm actually at the New
York Public Library now

01:00:03.710 --> 01:00:06.283
where I'm not doing research.

01:00:07.840 --> 01:00:12.840
And so on the one hand
that gives me some sense

01:00:15.000 --> 01:00:17.680
of discipline which I
think is really important

01:00:17.680 --> 01:00:21.800
like sticking with it even if
it's like inhabiting boredom.

01:00:21.800 --> 01:00:25.520
I think it's a very important
part of being a writer.

01:00:25.520 --> 01:00:27.003
I think it's crucial.

01:00:29.220 --> 01:00:33.080
But it's not research
in the traditional sense

01:00:33.080 --> 01:00:38.080
because I am not out to
corroborate some contextual facts

01:00:38.930 --> 01:00:41.890
or trying to make my post more accurate.

01:00:41.890 --> 01:00:44.720
To give you like very quick
examples, the kind of stuff

01:00:44.720 --> 01:00:47.050
that I'm looking for it's very weird.

01:00:47.050 --> 01:00:52.050
I'm looking for instance for
little, how can I put it?

01:00:54.580 --> 01:00:57.940
Little verbal prompts
for example, in the case

01:00:57.940 --> 01:01:02.940
of the 19th century that I
think we'll do a lot more work

01:01:04.030 --> 01:01:08.600
than really figuring out what
happened truly blow by blow

01:01:08.600 --> 01:01:11.090
at the whatever Mountain
Meadows Massacre which

01:01:11.090 --> 01:01:12.620
is important to know.

01:01:12.620 --> 01:01:15.680
But I think if you find that little word

01:01:16.940 --> 01:01:21.940
that will almost alchemically
conjure up a whole universe

01:01:22.640 --> 01:01:25.680
that is worth more than all
the fact checking in the world.

01:01:25.680 --> 01:01:30.030
And I get to those words
by just reading a lot

01:01:30.030 --> 01:01:30.863
while I'm writing.

01:01:30.863 --> 01:01:33.680
I know a lot of writer friends
don't even read fiction

01:01:33.680 --> 01:01:36.723
as they write their own
fiction, I'm the opposite.

01:01:37.960 --> 01:01:40.500
Like I read all of Dickens
while writing "In The Distance"

01:01:40.500 --> 01:01:45.450
and that somehow gave a sense
of 19th Century language

01:01:45.450 --> 01:01:48.890
that you can only get that
way just by being immersed in

01:01:48.890 --> 01:01:52.270
that world and I don't think
that's necessarily research.

01:01:52.270 --> 01:01:54.020
- No, I love this distinction.

01:01:54.020 --> 01:01:56.370
It's very useful to me personally.

01:01:56.370 --> 01:01:58.940
I'm gonna ask one question
from the audience and then

01:01:58.940 --> 01:02:00.950
that's probably all we have time for.

01:02:00.950 --> 01:02:01.783
- Sorry.

01:02:03.560 --> 01:02:06.240
- No, we wanted to hear
your wonderful talk.

01:02:06.240 --> 01:02:09.560
We had a member of the audience
ask what advice do you have

01:02:09.560 --> 01:02:13.270
for authors who are setting
stories even partially outside

01:02:13.270 --> 01:02:16.410
their own cultural experience,
in nations they don't know

01:02:16.410 --> 01:02:17.920
or among people whose identities

01:02:17.920 --> 01:02:20.750
the author doesn't know, yeah.

01:02:20.750 --> 01:02:22.520
- Yeah, that's a really
complicated question.

01:02:22.520 --> 01:02:27.301
I think and it's something
that I deal with myself.

01:02:27.301 --> 01:02:32.220
I don't find that I write
necessarily about myself

01:02:32.220 --> 01:02:33.700
and my environment.

01:02:33.700 --> 01:02:36.570
I think the first question
is why do you need

01:02:36.570 --> 01:02:37.630
to tell this story?

01:02:37.630 --> 01:02:42.370
I think that's the question
that you need to ask yourself

01:02:42.370 --> 01:02:44.810
and be dead honest with yourself about it.

01:02:44.810 --> 01:02:49.810
Why do you need to go
into this world when it

01:02:50.230 --> 01:02:52.950
is not given to you?

01:02:52.950 --> 01:02:56.980
And I think that the
second element is never

01:02:56.980 --> 01:03:01.980
to be a tourist in that
world and to really try

01:03:04.050 --> 01:03:05.527
to understand it.

01:03:05.527 --> 01:03:08.690
And if you understand
why you need to go there,

01:03:08.690 --> 01:03:13.670
chances are that maybe you
will also understand why

01:03:13.670 --> 01:03:15.230
you were drawn to it in the first place

01:03:15.230 --> 01:03:18.190
but I think it's a longer answer of course

01:03:18.190 --> 01:03:21.370
and I understand it has a
lot of political connotations

01:03:21.370 --> 01:03:24.200
but I think we should all be brave enough

01:03:24.200 --> 01:03:28.710
to venture outside our
world and write about others

01:03:28.710 --> 01:03:31.580
because otherwise
literature will be reduced

01:03:31.580 --> 01:03:32.773
to a selfie in a way.

01:03:34.370 --> 01:03:35.877
- I love that distinction of the tourists.

01:03:35.877 --> 01:03:37.190
I do wanna get to one more question

01:03:37.190 --> 01:03:39.700
if you're patient enough
Hernan to do one more.

01:03:39.700 --> 01:03:40.797
- Of course.

01:03:40.797 --> 01:03:43.810
- Judge Calabresi who is here did
ask a question and I'd love

01:03:43.810 --> 01:03:46.450
to hear the answer to and he
said, as you were talking,

01:03:46.450 --> 01:03:48.810
I kept thinking about my
reaction that seeing a work

01:03:48.810 --> 01:03:51.360
of visual art and how similar that was.

01:03:51.360 --> 01:03:53.710
Do you feel is there a difference?

01:03:53.710 --> 01:03:55.360
- Excuse me, can you repeat that?

01:03:56.270 --> 01:03:57.483
Oh, it's here, okay.

01:04:00.210 --> 01:04:02.123
Oh yes, no, absolutely.

01:04:05.110 --> 01:04:07.580
And thank you Judge Calabresi
for being here by the way.

01:04:07.580 --> 01:04:10.850
It's a great honor I didn't
know you were in the audience

01:04:13.090 --> 01:04:16.200
and for making this series possible.

01:04:16.200 --> 01:04:19.040
Definitely, I think it's very important

01:04:19.040 --> 01:04:23.350
to have almost like a cenesthetic
approach to literature.

01:04:23.350 --> 01:04:27.380
I think visual vividness is all important

01:04:27.380 --> 01:04:32.380
but I'm also very interested
in the auditive dimension

01:04:32.680 --> 01:04:37.010
of pros and how evocative sounds may be.

01:04:37.010 --> 01:04:40.070
I think literature gives
us this opportunity

01:04:40.070 --> 01:04:44.900
and you Meghan as a
poet know all about this

01:04:44.900 --> 01:04:49.090
but just the way a word looks and sounds

01:04:49.090 --> 01:04:51.040
and how it interacts with its meaning

01:04:51.040 --> 01:04:54.893
is such a beautiful thing
to use to one's advantage.

01:04:56.170 --> 01:04:58.310
- Well, this has been really wonderful.

01:04:58.310 --> 01:04:59.970
Thank you so much Hernan.

01:04:59.970 --> 01:05:01.740
Thank you all for coming today.

01:05:01.740 --> 01:05:04.700
I know we're sad not to be
in a physical space together

01:05:04.700 --> 01:05:07.860
but we're really glad to be in
this virtual space together.

01:05:07.860 --> 01:05:10.630
I'm delighted again to tell
you the talk will be published

01:05:10.630 --> 01:05:12.670
in the summer issue of The Yale Review.

01:05:12.670 --> 01:05:15.210
You can subscribe at our
website or pre-order the issue

01:05:15.210 --> 01:05:16.780
if you're interested.

01:05:16.780 --> 01:05:19.320
And thank you again to Alice Kaplan and

01:05:19.320 --> 01:05:22.760
the Whitney Humanities
Center for hosting this event

01:05:22.760 --> 01:05:24.730
and having us all here.

01:05:24.730 --> 01:05:26.976
Thank you everyone have
a wonderful evening.

01:05:26.976 --> 01:05:30.309
- Thank you Meghan, thank you everyone.

